{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon EKS enhances cluster governance with new IAM condition keys - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) has introduced seven new IAM condition keys for managing cluster creation and configuration APIs. These keys enhance governance controls through IAM policies and Service Control Policies (SCPs), allowing organizations to enforce security and compliance requirements consistently across all clusters. The new keys include:\n\n1. eks:endpointPublicAccess: Enforces private-only API endpoints.\n2. eks:endpointPrivateAccess: Requires private API endpoints.\n3. eks:encryptionConfigProviderKeyArns: Mandates the use of customer-managed AWS KMS keys for secrets encryption.\n4. eks:kubernetesVersion: Restricts clusters to approved Kubernetes versions.\n5. eks:deletionProtection: Mandates deletion protection for production workloads.\n6. eks:controlPlaneScalingTier: Specifies control plane scaling tiers.\n7. eks:zonalShiftEnabled: Enables zonal shift capabilities for high availability.\n\nThese condition keys apply to CreateCluster, UpdateClusterConfig, UpdateClusterVersion, and AssociateEncryptionConfig APIs, and integrate seamlessly with AWS Organizations SCPs for centralized governance across accounts. The new IAM condition keys are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon EKS is available at no additional charge. For more information, refer to the Amazon EKS User Guide and the Service Authorization Reference for Amazon EKS, as well as the AWS Organizations documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/04/amazon-eks-iam-condition-keys/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"rds-sqlserver","skill_description":"Provides connectivity, authentication, and troubleshooting guidance for Amazon RDS for SQL Server. Applicable when users ask about SSMS times out connecting from EC2, Cannot generate SSPI context with Windows auth, connect RDS SQL Server from Lambda with pymssql, auth_scheme shows NTLM instead of KERBEROS on ECS Fargate, SSM tunnel to RDS SQL Server from laptop, port 1433 security group, TrustServerCertificate=True for localhost tunnels, SPN MSSQLSvc, AWS Managed Microsoft AD, CNAME not RDS endpoint for Kerberos, tds_version='7.4', encryption='require', port-as-string for pymssql, Secrets Manager credential caching in Lambda, error 18456 login failed. Covers Python (pymssql, pyodbc), .NET (Microsoft.Data.SqlClient), Java (JDBC mssql-jdbc), Node.js (tedious), IAM auth via RDS Proxy, and VPC/ECS/EKS/Lambda deployment.\n\nServices: rds, secretsmanager, ec2, lambda, ecs, eks, directoryservice\nTasks: connect, configure, troubleshoot, deploy, authenticate, create, provision, modify\nPersona: developer, devops, dba\nWorkload: application, legacy-migration, .net, java, python, nodejs","skill_name":"rds-sqlserver"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS Batch now supports Amazon EKS access entry authentication - AWS","context":"AWS Batch now supports Amazon EKS access entry authentication for compute environments. This new feature simplifies cluster setup and authentication lifecycle management by allowing AWS Batch to authenticate to Kubernetes clusters through the access entry mechanism. To enable this feature, use the CreateComputeEnvironment or UpdateComputeEnvironment APIs to set accessEntry.desiredState to ENABLED on all AWS Batch compute environments targeting the cluster. AWS Batch creates one access entry per cluster and associates the AWSBatchClusterPolicy with it. Access entries can be configured through the AWS CLI, AWS SDKs, or AWS Management Console. This feature is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Batch is available. For more information, refer to the Amazon EKS access entry authentication section in the AWS Batch User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/aws-batch-access-entries/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles","skill_description":"Configures EC2 instances to securely call AWS services by creating and attaching IAM roles via instance profiles, eliminating hardcoded credentials. Use when an EC2 instance needs permissions to access AWS services like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, or CloudWatch through temporary credentials.\n\nServices: ec2, iam\nTasks: configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security","skill_name":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"amazon-ses","skill_description":"Guides Amazon SES onboarding for domain-based email sending. Covers identity configuration, production access, an optional first test send, and troubleshooting setup, authentication, or sending failures. Use when setting up SES, resuming incomplete onboarding, or leaving the sandbox. Does not cover inbound email or Mail Manager, SMS/voice, WhatsApp, SNS, Pinpoint, or WorkMail.\n\nServices: ses, route53\nTasks: onboard, configure, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: email, messaging","skill_name":"amazon-ses"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS docs on IAM role path handling in EKS aws-auth ConfigMap"}
